from part1 import danger, caution, tip, check, table, fig

def build(D):
    h = []
    h.append('<h1 class="part" id="p4"><small>Part 4 · Main project</small>Making Your Own Diesel Fuel</h1>')
    h.append('<p>Diesel engines run tractors, trucks, boats and the most durable generators. When the fuel supply stops, there are three realistic ways to keep them running: <b>biodiesel</b> made from vegetable oil or animal fat (the best option — works in almost any diesel engine), <b>straight vegetable oil (SVO)</b> in suitable engines with modifications, and <b>wood gas</b> as a partial substitute. This part focuses on biodiesel, made by a reaction called <b>transesterification</b>: fat or oil is reacted with methanol, using lye as a catalyst, to make methyl esters (biodiesel) and glycerin.</p>')
    h.append(danger("Read before you start: methanol and lye are dangerous", "<b>Methanol</b> (wood alcohol) is <b>poisonous</b> — as little as 30 mL (2 tablespoons) swallowed can blind or kill, and it is absorbed through the skin and lungs. It is <b>highly flammable</b>, burns with a nearly invisible flame in daylight, and its vapor can flash back to a distant spark. <b>Lye</b> (sodium hydroxide NaOH or potassium hydroxide KOH) causes severe chemical burns and permanent blindness within seconds. <b>Methoxide</b> (lye dissolved in methanol) combines both hazards and is worse than either alone.<br><b>Rules:</b> work outdoors or in a strongly ventilated shed with no ignition sources (no pilot lights, smoking, sparking motors or open heating elements near methanol); wear <b>chemical splash goggles plus face shield, nitrile or butyl gloves, long sleeves and apron</b>; mix methoxide in a closed container; keep a running hose or 20+ litres of clean water for flushing skin and eyes (flush 15+ minutes); keep a dry-chemical (ABC) fire extinguisher; label everything; keep children and animals away. Never use aluminum, tin or zinc containers with lye or methoxide — use HDPE plastic or stainless steel. Lye is added <i>to</i> methanol, never the reverse, and never mix lye with water in a closed container (it gets very hot)."))
    h.append('<h2 id="feed">4.1 Feedstocks</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Feedstock", "Pros", "Cons"], [
        ["New vegetable oil (canola, sunflower, soybean…)", "Easiest reaction, low free fatty acids (FFA); consistent", "Competes with food; must grow or buy it"],
        ["Waste fryer oil (restaurants, home)", "Free/cheap; huge supply while restaurants operate", "Variable FFA and water — must titrate every batch; filter food bits"],
        ["Animal fat (lard, tallow, poultry fat, fish oil)", "From butchering/rendering you would do anyway", "Biodiesel gels at 13–16 °C (55–61 °F); best in warm climates or blended"]]))
    h.append('<p><b>Filtering and dewatering.</b> Strain used oil through cloth or a 50–100 µm filter while warm. Water ruins the reaction (it makes soap instead of fuel). Let oil settle warm for 24 h and draw off from above the bottom layer; or heat it gently to about 60 °C, hold, and let water settle out. Test: heat a small sample in a pan to ~120 °C — crackling or popping means water remains. Never boil large volumes of oil indoors.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="titrate">4.2 Titration: how much lye?</h2>')
    h.append('<p>New oil needs a fixed amount of catalyst. Used oil contains free fatty acids that consume extra lye, so you must <b>titrate</b> (measure the acidity) each batch. This is the Journey to Forever method:</p>')
    h.append('<ol><li>Make a <b>reference lye solution</b>: dissolve exactly 1 g NaOH in 1 litre of distilled water (0.1% solution). Keep sealed; remake if old. (If using KOH, dissolve 1 g KOH per litre instead.)</li><li>In a small jar, dissolve <b>1 mL of warm oil</b> in <b>10 mL of isopropyl alcohol</b> (99% if possible). Warm gently and stir until clear.</li><li>Add 2 drops of <b>phenolphthalein</b> indicator (or use a digital pH meter to pH 8.5).</li><li>Using a syringe or graduated dropper, add the reference solution <b>drop by drop</b>, swirling, until the liquid turns <b>pink and stays pink for 10–15 seconds</b>.</li><li>The number of <b>mL of reference solution used = extra grams of lye per litre of oil</b>. Repeat 3 times and average.</li></ol>')
    h.append('<div class="formula"><span class="eq">NaOH (g) = (base + titration mL) × litres of oil</span><span class="eq">KOH (g) = (base + titration mL) × litres of oil ÷ KOH purity</span><span class="eq">Methanol = 20% of oil volume (200 mL per litre)</span><b>Base amount</b> (catalyst needed for perfectly fresh oil): Journey to Forever’s classic figure is <b>3.5 g/L NaOH</b> (≈ 4.9 g/L pure KOH). Many current small-scale producers use a higher base of about <b>5.5 g/L NaOH or 7 g/L KOH</b> for more complete conversion of used oil. Start with the lower base on a 1-litre test batch; if it fails the 3/27 test, step up. Example (classic base): 50 L of fryer oil titrates at 2.5 mL. NaOH = (3.5 + 2.5) × 50 = <b>300 g</b>; methanol = 0.20 × 50 = <b>10 L</b>. With 90%-pure KOH (titrated with the KOH solution, here 3.5 mL): (4.9 + 3.5) × 50 ÷ 0.90 = <b>467 g</b>. Use fresh, dry lye — it absorbs moisture and CO₂ from air; keep it sealed. Oil titrating above about 6–8 mL has too much free fatty acid for this one-step method; blend it with better oil or use a two-stage (acid-base) process from the references.</div>')
    h.append(tip("NaOH or KOH?", "NaOH is cheaper and needs less by weight, but dissolves slowly in methanol. KOH dissolves faster, works better with high-FFA oil, and its glycerin byproduct can become fertilizer (potassium). Practice first with 1 litre ‘mini-batches’ in a sealed plastic bottle before scaling up."))
    h.append('<h2 id="process">4.3 The process step by step</h2>')
    h.append(fig(D.biodiesel_flow(), "<b>Figure 9. Biodiesel process flow.</b> One litre of oil yields roughly one litre of biodiesel (≈ 90–98% by volume) plus about 0.2 L of crude glycerin layer."))
    h.append('<ol><li><b>Heat the oil</b> in the reactor (a closed steel drum or an old electric water heater tank with an external pump are common designs) to <b>48–55 °C (120–130 °F)</b>. Methanol boils at 64.7 °C (148 °F) — stay well below it. Turn off and unplug any exposed heating element before adding methanol unless it is fully immersed and designed for it.</li><li><b>Make methoxide</b>: pour the measured methanol into a dedicated HDPE container, then slowly add the weighed lye. Close the lid and swirl/shake (or use a sealed mixer) until the lye is completely dissolved — 10–30 minutes. It will warm up. Wear full PPE; point the opening away from your face when venting.</li><li><b>React</b>: transfer methoxide into the warm oil with a sealed pump or closed funnel while mixing. Mix vigorously but without splashing or sucking in air (a pump recirculating the liquid or a slow paddle mixer) for <b>about 1 hour</b>, keeping the reactor closed and vented away from people.</li><li><b>Settle</b> for 8–24 hours. Dark glycerin sinks to the bottom; amber biodiesel floats on top.</li><li><b>Drain the glycerin</b> from a bottom valve into a labeled container. It contains much of the excess methanol and lye — treat it as hazardous (see byproducts).</li><li><b>Wash</b> to remove soap, methanol, lye and glycerin traces. First wash gently: mist water over the surface or bubble air through a water layer (an aquarium air pump with stones) so you don’t create emulsions. Drain water after it settles; repeat 3–4 times until the wash water is clear and near pH 7. A few mL of vinegar in the first wash helps neutralize lye.</li><li><b>Dry</b>: heat the washed biodiesel to ~50 °C in an open-topped container, or stand it in the sun in clear containers, until it goes from cloudy to <b>crystal clear</b> (you should be able to read newsprint through a jar of it).</li><li><b>Test</b> (below), then <b>filter</b> through a 5-micron (or finer) filter into clean, dry storage containers.</li></ol>')
    h.append('<h2 id="testing">4.4 Quality testing</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Test", "How", "Pass", "If it fails"], [
        ["<b>3/27 conversion test</b>", "In a clear jar mix 3 mL biodiesel with 27 mL methanol; shake; let stand 30 min (oil and methanol at ~20 °C)", "Clear single liquid; nothing settles out", "Any droplets or layer on the bottom = unreacted oil. Re-process with a second, smaller reaction (e.g., ~25% of the original lye + methanol)."],
        ["<b>Wash test</b>", "Shake 150 mL biodiesel with 150 mL warm water in a jar for 10 s; let settle", "Separates into clear layers within ~30 min", "Thick white emulsion/milky middle layer = soap or incomplete reaction"],
        ["<b>Clarity / water</b>", "Look through a clear jar; ‘hot pan’ test for water", "Bright, clear, no haze or droplets; no crackle", "Dry longer; re-settle; filter"],
        ["<b>pH</b>", "Paper or meter on final wash water", "~7", "Wash again"]]))
    h.append(caution("Byproducts and waste", "<b>Glycerin layer</b>: contains methanol (toxic, flammable) and lye. Store sealed and labeled. Once the methanol is safely evaporated outdoors in an open, ventilated, ignition-free area, KOH glycerin can be composted in small amounts, made into degreasing soap, or used as a firestarter fuel for outdoor burners. <b>Wash water</b> is soapy and alkaline — never pour it into streams or wells; let it sit in an open container to evaporate methanol, then dispose away from water sources."))
    h.append(tip("Engines and biodiesel", "Pure biodiesel (B100) runs in almost any diesel engine without modification. It is a solvent: in an old engine it loosens tank deposits, so <b>carry spare fuel filters</b> and change them after the first few tanks. It softens natural rubber hoses and seals found in many engines made before the mid-1990s — replace with Viton/fluorocarbon parts. Biodiesel has ~8% less energy per gallon than petroleum diesel, so expect slightly lower power and mileage. Modern common-rail engines with particulate filters prefer blends (B20 or less)."))
    h.append('<h2 id="supply">4.5 Long-term supply: methanol and lye</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Oil is renewable; <b>methanol and lye are the real bottlenecks</b>. Each 100 L of biodiesel needs about 20 L of methanol and 0.4–1 kg of lye. Stockpile them: methanol keeps for years in sealed steel drums or HDPE containers stored cool and dark; NaOH/KOH keep indefinitely if sealed airtight. Methanol can be made by destructive distillation of hardwood (the original ‘wood alcohol’) but yields are small and the product impure — not a practical home source. <b>Ethanol</b> can replace methanol (making ethyl esters), but it must be nearly water-free (≥ 99%, i.e., dried beyond what simple distillation gives, e.g., with 3A molecular sieves), uses KOH, and the reaction and separation are more difficult. Lye leached from wood ash is weak and impure and is not reliable for biodiesel. For a family, a 55-gal (208 L) drum of methanol makes roughly 1,000 L (265 gal) of fuel.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="crops">4.6 Growing and pressing oil crops</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Crop", "Oil yield (approx.)", "Seed oil %", "Climate / region fit", "Notes"], [
        ["Canola / rapeseed", "~127 gal/acre (≈1,190 L/ha)", "40–44%", "Cool, short seasons: Prairies, Great Lakes north, Northeast, Pacific NW, Peace River", "Best North American biodiesel crop; good cold-flow. Winter types in the South"],
        ["Sunflower (oilseed)", "~100 gal/acre (≈950 L/ha)", "40–45%", "Plains, Midwest, California, irrigated SW, Northeast", "Easy to grow and press by hand; drought-tolerant; birds love it"],
        ["Safflower", "~80 gal/acre (≈780 L/ha)", "35–45%", "Dry, hot: California, Montana/Sask., Great Basin, N. Mexico (Sonora, Sinaloa)", "Very drought-tolerant; spiny"],
        ["Soybean", "~48 gal/acre (≈450 L/ha)", "18–20%", "Midwest, Southeast, eastern Plains, S. Ontario", "Low oil; hard to press without extruder; meal is excellent feed"],
        ["Camelina", "~60 gal/acre (≈580 L/ha)", "35–40%", "Cold, dry, poor soils: northern Plains, Rockies, PNW interior, subarctic valleys", "Short 85–100 day season; low inputs"],
        ["Peanut", "~110 gal/acre (≈1,060 L/ha)", "45–50%", "Southeast, Florida, Texas, Mexico", "Must be shelled first; also food"],
        ["Oil palm", "~600+ gal/acre (≈5,900 L/ha)", "(fruit) ~45–50%", "Humid tropics only: S. Mexico, Central America", "Highest yield; tree crop (3–4 yrs to bear); fat gels ~13–16 °C"],
        ["Coconut", "~280 gal/acre (≈2,700 L/ha)", "(copra) 60–65%", "Tropical coasts, Caribbean, S. Florida", "Dry the meat to copra, then press"],
        ["Jatropha / castor", "~200 / ~150 gal/acre", "30–50%", "Dry tropics and subtropics: Mexico, Central America, Caribbean", "<b>Seeds are poisonous</b> (castor contains ricin) — non-food fuel crops; wear gloves; keep from children and livestock"]]))
    h.append('<p class="small">Yields are typical commercial figures (Journey to Forever vegetable-oil yield table; extension data). Small-scale screw presses recover about 60–80% of the oil in the seed; expect less in the first years.</p>')
    h.append('<p><b>Pressing.</b> A hand-cranked screw (expeller) press processes roughly 2–5 kg of seed per hour; small electric presses 5–20 kg/h (and need 1–2 kW — a good use of daytime solar or hydro surplus). Seed must be clean and dry (about 6–9% moisture for sunflower and canola). Let pressed oil settle for 1–2 weeks and decant, or filter. The leftover <b>press cake (meal)</b> is valuable protein feed for livestock (and food-grade for some crops) — oilseed farming pays twice. Rough land planning: 1 acre (0.4 ha) of canola or sunflower ≈ 80–120 gal (300–450 L) of fuel per year after small-press losses — enough for a small tractor’s season or about 400–700 hours of a small diesel generator at light load.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="svo">4.7 Straight vegetable oil (SVO / WVO)</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Filtered vegetable oil can be burned directly in some diesel engines without making biodiesel — no methanol or lye needed. But oil is 10–20 times thicker than diesel at room temperature, so it must be heated to about <b>70–80 °C (160–175 °F)</b> before the injection pump to atomize properly.</p>')
    h.append('<ul><li><b>Best engines:</b> older, mechanically injected, <b>indirect-injection (IDI)</b> diesels (e.g., older Mercedes, Volkswagen, many tractors) and slow-speed stationary engines such as Lister-type single-cylinder engines. Inline (piston) injection pumps tolerate oil better than rotary pumps.</li><li><b>Avoid</b> modern common-rail and direct-injection engines with emissions systems; they coke injectors, glaze cylinders and contaminate engine oil.</li><li><b>Two-tank system:</b> start and warm up on diesel or biodiesel, switch to heated oil once the coolant is hot, and switch back to diesel a few minutes before shutting down to purge the injectors.</li><li><b>Fuel prep:</b> settle, dewater, and filter to <b>1–5 microns</b>. Change engine oil more often and watch for thickening (polymerized vegetable oil in the crankcase).</li><li>Thinning oil with 10–30% diesel, kerosene or biodiesel helps in older stationary engines but does not replace heating in vehicles.</li></ul>')
    h.append('<h2 id="cold">4.8 Cold-weather gelling</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Fuel", "Typical cloud point (wax/crystals begin)"], [
        ["Canola biodiesel", "≈ −3 °C (27 °F)"], ["Soybean biodiesel", "≈ 0–2 °C (32–36 °F)"], ["Sunflower biodiesel", "≈ 1–3 °C (34–37 °F)"],
        ["Used fryer-oil biodiesel", "≈ 0–10 °C (32–50 °F), varies"], ["Tallow, lard, palm biodiesel", "≈ 13–16 °C (55–61 °F)"],
        ["#2 diesel (winterized varies)", "≈ −10 to −20 °C (14 to −4 °F)"], ["#1 diesel / kerosene", "≈ −40 °C (−40 °F)"]]))
    h.append('<p>Fixes: blend biodiesel with winter #2, #1 diesel or kerosene (e.g., B20 in deep cold); use canola rather than animal fat for winter; save tallow fuel for summer or for warm climates; park or store fuel in a heated building; use fuel-filter heaters, tank heaters and engine block heaters; add cold-flow additives labeled for biodiesel. SVO is unusable in cold without full heating of tank, lines and filter.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="store-bd">4.9 Storing homemade fuel</h2>')
    h.append(check("Fuel storage checklist", ["Clean, dry, opaque containers (HDPE, steel; no copper, brass, zinc or galvanized — they speed oxidation)", "Fill containers nearly full to limit air; seal tightly", "Store cool and dark; use biodiesel within ~6–12 months (add an antioxidant such as TBHQ-based stabilizer for longer)", "Drain water from the bottom regularly; add a diesel biocide if microbial ‘slime’ appears", "Label each batch with date, feedstock and 3/27 test result; use oldest first", "Filter (≤ 5 µm) when filling the tank; carry spare filters"]))
    h.append('<h2 id="woodgas">4.10 Wood gasification (brief)</h2>')
    h.append('<p>A <b>wood gasifier</b> heats wood chunks or charcoal with limited air, producing a combustible gas (mostly carbon monoxide, hydrogen and nitrogen) that can run engines. Over a million vehicles ran on wood gas in Europe during World War II. A U.S. government design is published in the FEMA/Oak Ridge report <i>Construction of a Simplified Wood Gas Generator for Fueling Internal Combustion Engines in a Petroleum Emergency</i> (1989). Key points:</p>')
    h.append('<ul><li><b>Spark-ignition (gasoline) engines</b> can run 100% on wood gas at about 50–70% of their gasoline power. <b>Diesel engines</b> can run in ‘dual-fuel’ mode, with wood gas replacing roughly 60–90% of the diesel; they still need a diesel pilot injection to ignite.</li><li>Fuel: dry (&lt; 20% moisture), uniform hardwood chunks or charcoal; roughly 1–2 kg (2–4 lb) of dry wood per kWh of engine output.</li><li>Gas must be cooled and filtered; tar and ash will wreck an engine quickly.</li></ul>')
    h.append(danger("Wood gas is mostly carbon monoxide", "Wood gas is lethal to breathe. Operate gasifiers outdoors only, never in garages or enclosed spaces, keep CO detectors nearby, and never light or vent gas near buildings. Gasifiers are hot enough to start fires; keep clearances and an extinguisher on hand."))
    return "\n".join(h)
